{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"update-stage25.json\", \"summary\": \"Focused source item for the remaining budget: what counts as existing state when a caller decides a node is new, and whether that lines up with what the constructor restores (valid:true).\\n\\nRecorded behaviour and explained investigation, no defect claim:\\n`HasExistingState` (api.go:460-494) is documented as reporting logs, knowledge of a current term, or any snapshot, and the implementation checks exactly that — a stable current term above zero returns true, a \\\"not found\\\" term is ignored while other term errors propagate, a log last index above zero returns true, and a non-empty snapshot list returns true. `NewRaft`'s documentation (api.go:496-500) states that old state (snapshots, logs, peers) is restored when the node is constructed, so a caller that respects the helper will not bootstrap over data the constructor would recover; the captured test expectation (raft_test.go:255-295) covers both directions, a fresh node reporting no state and a joined node reporting state.\\n\\nThe one durable component the helper does not consult directly — the persisted vote — is closed by the durable ordering of the two write paths rather than left as a gap: the election path raises the term (raft.go:1983) before persisting the vote (raft.go:2021), and the vote-handling path adopts the raised term (raft.go:1668) before persisting the vote (raft.go:1726), so a stored vote cannot exist without a non-zero current term that the first check covers. The remaining asymmetry is conservative rather than unsafe: a used-but-cleared log store, or a custom store reporting a non-zero last index for a fresh node, makes the helper report existing state, erring towards recovery. Limitations state that this is source-only, that the conservative direction was not exercised against a real store, and that the vote-order argument applies to the write paths present in this revision.\"}"}}
